Some of the concrete facts and mechanical procedures that the author lays out are that it captures every detail, it is dependent on the ambient light, prints in color are not yet successfully produced yet so photos are mostly just in one color, and that the number of different processes for photography are innumerable. Andrew takes these technical realities and concludes that simply reproducing what the eye is capable of seeing is not art and it requires human intention to interpret an image. To communicate is the true purpose of art. The difference between an artist and a routine machine operator is that an artist uses their judgement, taste, and the ability to interpret while the machine operator simply follows a hard set of machine instructions. When Andrew says “wait and watch” (p. 8)  in reference to the art of photography he means that a photographer that is also an artist will be patient and make deliberate choices to get the right shot. The photographer will also have a deep understanding of art principles and how light interacts with the lens. Andrew suggests that photography should be used as a tool by artists to support their study of traditional media by using photos as a reference such as studying a photo of an art work created by the old masters or using it to study subjects that were previously impossible to study like the waves of the ocean. Artistic feeling is internal sensibility that tells an artist what to include, to focus on, and how to frame a composition. Artistic feeling transforms the act of taking a photo in to a set of deliberate choices to make an everyday sight in to a beautiful work of art that tells a story or communicates a message. The photographer may choose a specific time of day, an unusual angle, or particular method to achieve the desired final image.When Emerson defines art as “Nature passed through the alembic of man” (p.12) he is in effect defining art as the result of man interpreting the natural world in a visual media by making choices to filter out extraneous detail and focusing on a subject or depicting how the scene feels. Andrew agrees with Emerson, he writes “it is a selection of idealised Forms” (p.12). Hamerton asserts that photography can never advance beyond the recording of plain facts because he believes that photography cannot exclude extraneous details. To be classified as a fine art under Hamerton's criteria the medium must be under the complete control of the artist so that they are able to make decisions on where detail can be shown to draw the viewer's attention. Hamerton’s and Emerson’s view on photography as a form of art differ because Hamerton’s critiques completely denies photography as a form of art because the camera is rigid in its physical mechanism while Emerson’s alembic points to the fact that while the camera is rigid the choice of how to use it can affect the final product so photography would then count as a form of art. I would consider digital art as a cousin of traditional media because it can closely mimic traditional media with added features like the ability to reverse unintentional strokes and lines and can combine different media styles that would not be possible in physical media. Ai art would qualify as a distant relative because the prompts can be identical but the output that the ai would create would be different every time even if it was the same model. Ai art would be more closely related to the language arts than the visual arts, but it should be in its own technological category. Ai should be admitted into the family of fine arts because the fine arts are defined by the ability of the artist to be an interpreter and filter and that is no more apparent than in the prompt writer's ability to dictate the prompt. It was clear that Mr. Allen complied with all the rules of the competition and was not obscuring the fact that his entry was made with ai so in my book it is completely fair but should be in a separate category. The composing and recomposing of the text prompt to get the image to meet your artist feeling exemplifies Emerson's belief that art is “nature passed through the alembic of man” (p.12) because all of the training data of the ai is directly or indirectly derived from nature and is passed through the alembic of the prompt writer.
